@node Appendix. ASCII code comparison: ASCII67, ATASCII, PETSCII, modern ASCII (no ←, ↑)
@appendix ASCII code comparison

This appendix compares different ASCII variants used across various
platforms supported by EIGHTBOL.

@table @asis
@item ASCII67 (1963 version) - Early ASCII without lowercase
@item ATASCII - Atari ASCII variant used in 8-bit computers
@item PETSCII - Commodore PET/C64 variant with graphical characters
@item Modern ASCII - Standard ASCII without the ← and ↑ control codes
@end table

Oh, look. No table. I guess that didn't get written yet.

Hint: there's 128 codes in ASCII, and 128 or 256 in ATASCII and PETSCII,
and the later ANSI ASCII Unicode version is also 128 characters.
So there's  no way this table  can be less  than 128 rows long  and it's
probably got  to be much longer,  like 256+, to include  characters that
may only be found in one character set (like pi).

